r) -> Option<i32> {\n    let bytes = s.as_bytes();\n    if bytes.is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    \n    let mut i = 0;\n    let mut negative = false;\n    \n    if bytes[0] == b'+' {\n        i = 1;\n        if i >= bytes.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n    } else if bytes[0] == b'-' {\n        negative = true;\n        i = 1;\n        if i >= bytes.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    let mut result: i32 = 0;\n    \n    while i < bytes.len() {\n        let digit = bytes[i];\n        if digit < b'0' || digit > b'9' {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let d = (digit - b'0') as i32;\n        \n        if negative {\n            // Check for underflow: result * 10 - d < i32::MIN\n            // Equivalent to: result < (i32::MIN + d) / 10\n            // But this is complex. Let me use a simpler check.\n            if result < i32::MIN / 10 || (result == i32::MIN / 10 && d > 8) {\n                // Wait, this doesn't work because result is positive.\n            }\n        } else {\n            if result > i32::MAX / 10 || (result == i32::MAX / 10 && d > 7) {\n                return None;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        result = result * 10 + d;\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    \n    if negative {\n        Some(-result)\n    } else {\n        Some(result)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the issue is that `result` is always positive (I'm parsing the absolute value). So the check `result < i32::MIN / 10` doesn't make sense (i32::MIN / 10 is negative).\n\nLet me fix this by parsing the number as a negative value from the start (if it's negative):\n\n```rust\nfn parse_i32(s: &str) -> Option<i32> {\n    let bytes = s.as_bytes();\n    if bytes.is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    \n    let mut i = 0;\n    let mut negative = false;\n    \n    if bytes[0] == b'+' {\n        i = 1;\n        if i >= bytes.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n    } else if bytes[0] == b'-' {\n        negative = true;\n        i = 1;\n        if i >= bytes.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    let mut result: i32 = 0;\n    \n    while i < bytes.len() {\n        let digit = bytes[i];\n        if digit < b'0' || digit > b'9' {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let d = (digit - b'0') as i32;\n        \n        if negative {\n            // result is negative (or zero)\n            // Check for underflow: result * 10 - d < i32::MIN\n            if result < i32::MIN / 10 || (result == i32::MIN / 10 && d > 8) {\n                return None;\n            }\n            result = result * 10 - d;\n        } else {\n            if result > i32::MAX / 10 || (result == i32::MAX / 10 && d > 7) {\n                return None;\n            }\n            result = result * 10 + d;\n        }\n        \n        i += 1;\n    }\n    \n    Some(result)\n}\n```\n\nWait, but `result` starts at 0. If `negative` is true, I'm doing `result = result * 10 - d`. So after the first digit, `result = -d`. After the second digit, `result = -d1 * 10 - d2`. etc.\n\nLet me verify with \"-2147483648\":\n- result = 0.\n- d = 2. result = 0 * 10 - 2 = -2.\n- d = 1. result = -2 * 10 - 1 = -21.\n- d = 4. result = -21 * 10 - 4 = -214.\n- ...\n- After parsing \"214748364\": result = -214748364.\n- d = 8. Check: result < i32::MIN / 10? -214748364 < -214748364? False. result == i32::MIN / 10? -214748364 == -214748364? True. d > 8? 8 > 8? False. So no overflow.\n- result = -214748364 * 10 - 8 = -2147483648. This is i32::MIN. Good.\n\nLet me verify with \"-2147483649\":\n- After parsing \"214748364\": result = -214748364.\n- d = 9. Check: result < i32::MIN / 10? False. result == i32::MIN / 10? True. d > 8? 9 > 8? True. Overflow. Return None. Good.\n\nLet me verify with \"2147483647\":\n- result = 0.\n- d = 2. result = 2.\n- ...\n- After parsing \"214748364\": result = 214748364.\n- d = 7. Check: result > i32::MAX / 10? 214748364 > 214748364? False. result == i32::MAX / 10? True. d > 7