d_in_range` function. It returns the index of the first element in the range [low, high]. If no element is found, it returns a.len().\n\n```rust\nfn find_in_range(a: &[i32], low: i32, high: i32) -> usize {\n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i < a.len() {\n        if a[i] >= low && a[i] <= high {\n            return i;\n        }\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    a.len()\n}\n```\n\nThis is correct.\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about the `solve_large` function more carefully. The algorithm is:\n\n1. Get sorted copy of a.\n2. For each chunk, find elements in the chunk range and pb them.\n3. For each element, rotate a to bring it to the top (choosing the direction that minimizes rotations).\n4. After pb-ing, rotate b to position the element within the chunk (largest at top).\n5. After all elements are in b, push them back.\n6. For the push-back phase, process chunks in reverse order (largest first).\n7. Within each chunk, pa elements (no rotations needed, since they're already positioned).\n8. Final rotation to put min on top.\n\nLet me implement this.\n\nFor step 4, I need to find the rank of the element within the chunk (0 = largest, 1 = second largest, etc.). Then rotate b to put it at position rank from the top.\n\n```rust\n// After pb-ing, the element is at the top of b.\n// Find its rank within the chunk.\nlet mut rank = 0;\nfor i in 1..b.len() {\n    // Check if b[i] is in the same chunk\n    if b[i] >= low && b[i] <= high {\n        if b[i] > val {\n            rank += 1;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Rotate b to put the element at position rank.\n// The element is at position 0. We need to move it to position rank.\n// rb rank times.\nfor _ in 0..rank {\n    rb(b);\n}\n```\n\nWait, but after rb-ing, the element moves down by 1 position. So after rb-ing rank times, the element is at position rank.\n\nBut wait, rb rotates the entire b. So after rb-ing rank times, the element is at position rank, and the elements at positions 0..rank-1 move up by 1.\n\nBut the elements at positions 0..rank-1 are the elements in the chunk that are larger than the new element. So after rb-ing, the chunk is sorted descending (largest at top).\n\nWait, let me think about this more carefully. After pb-ing, b has:\n- Position 0: the new element.\n- Positions 1..: elements from previous chunks and the current chunk.\n\nAfter rb-ing rank times, b has:\n- Positions 0..rank-1: the elements that were at positions 1..rank (which are the elements in the chunk that are larger than the new element).\n- Position rank: the new element.\n- Positions rank+1..: the remaining elements.\n\nSo the chunk is sorted descending (largest at top). \n\nBut wait, I need to also account for elements from previous chunks. They're below the current chunk. After rb-ing, they're shifted as well. But they're still below the current chunk.\n\nActually, no. After pb-ing, the new element is at the top. Elements from previous chunks are at the bottom. Elements from the current chunk (already in b) are between the new element and the previous chunks.\n\nAfter rb-ing rank times, the new element moves down by rank positions. The elements at positions 1..rank move up by 1. These elements are from the current chunk (larger than the new element).\n\nSo the current chunk is now sorted descending (largest at top). The previous chunks are below, in their original order.\n\nGood.\n\nBut wait, there's an issue. When I rb, I rotate the entire b. So elements from previous chunks are also rotated. After rb-ing rank times, elements from previous chunks are shifted by rank positions.\n\nBut since elements from previous chunks are below the current chunk, they're not affected by the positioning of the current chunk. They're just shifted.\n\nActually, they are affected. After rb-ing rank times, elements from previous chunks move up by rank positions (wrapping around). Wait, no. rb rotates the entire b. So the element at position b.len()-1 moves to position 0.