s in b are in random order, the average rotation cost is b.len()/4 per element. Total: sum of b.len()/4 for b.len() = 100..1 = 1237.5.\n\nTotal: 300 + 100 + 1237.5 + 100 + 50 = 1787.5.\n\nThat's over budget.\n\nHmm, so both approaches (positioning in b and not positioning) are over budget. I need a better algorithm.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The key insight for an efficient push_swap is to minimize the total rotation cost. One way to do this is to use the `rr` and `rrr` operations to rotate both stacks simultaneously.\n\nFor example, instead of ra + rb, use rr (1 op instead of 2). This saves 1 op per simultaneous rotation.\n\nLet me add support for `rr` and `rrr` in the algorithm. This can significantly reduce the operation count.\n\nBut this adds complexity. Let me first implement the basic algorithm and test it. If the operation count is too high, I'll add support for `rr` and `rrr`.\n\nActually, let me think about the operation count more carefully. My estimate of 1787.5 for n=100 is based on positioning elements relative to all elements in b. But if I position elements only within the current chunk, the cost is lower.\n\nLet me recalculate. When pushing elements from chunk 0:\n- b has only elements from chunk 0.\n- Positioning cost: sum of min(rank, chunk_size_so_far - rank) for each element.\n- For chunk_size=20: 47.5.\n\nWhen pushing elements from chunk 1:\n- b has elements from chunk 0 and chunk 1.\n- But I only position elements within chunk 1 (relative to other elements in chunk 1).\n- The positioning cost is the same: 47.5.\n\nWait, but when I rb or rrb, I rotate the entire b, including elements from chunk 0. So the cost is min(rank, b.len()-rank), not min(rank, chunk_size_so_far-rank).\n\nFor chunk 1, b.len() = 20 + chunk_size_so_far. So the cost is min(rank, 20 + chunk_size_so_far - rank).\n\nFor rank = 10, chunk_size_so_far = 10: min(10, 20 + 10 - 10) = min(10, 20) = 10.\n\nSo the cost is the same as min(rank, chunk_size_so_far - rank) for