ements within a chunk are not in random order. They're in the order they were pushed, which is the order they appeared in a.\n\nIf elements in a are random, then elements within a chunk are also random. So the estimate is correct.\n\nBut if elements in a are not random (e.g., reverse sorted), then elements within a chunk might be in a specific order. The rotation cost might be different.\n\nBut the grading uses random permutations, so the estimate should be correct.\n\nOK, let me implement the algorithm now. I'll use the chunk-based algorithm without positioning in b. The push-back phase will find the largest element in each chunk and rotate b to bring it to top.\n\nLet me write the complete code.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about the `solve_large` function more carefully. The algorithm is:\n\n1. Get sorted copy of a.\n2. For each chunk, find elements in the chunk range and pb them.\n3. For each element, rotate a to bring it to the top (choosing the direction that minimizes rotations).\n4. After all elements are in b, push them back.\n5. For the push-back phase, process chunks in reverse order (largest first).\n6. Within each chunk, find the largest element, rotate b to bring it to top, pa it. Repeat.\n7. Final rotation to put min on top.\n\nLet me implement this.\n\nFor step 6, I need to find the largest element in the chunk. I'll scan the chunk and find the position of the largest element. Then rotate b to bring it to top.\n\n```rust\n// For each chunk (largest first)\nfor chunk in (0..n_chunks).rev() {\n    let start = chunk * n / n_chunks;\n    let end = (chunk + 1) * n / n_chunks;\n    let low = sorted[start];\n    let high = sorted[end - 1];\n    \n    // Push all elements in this chunk range from b to a\n    let mut remaining = count_in_range(b, low, high);\n    while remaining > 0 {\n        // Find the largest element in this chunk\n        let mut max_pos = 0;\n        let mut max_val = b[0];\n        let mut i = 0;\n        while i < b.len() {\n            if b[i] >= low && b[i] <= high {\n                if b[i] > max_val {\n                    max_val = b[i];\n                    max_pos = i;\n                }\n            }\n            i += 1;\n        }\n        \n        // But wait, max_val might not be in the chunk. Let me fix this.\n        // Find the first element in the chunk, then find the max.\n        \n        // Actually, let me find the max among elements in the chunk.\n        let mut found = false;\n        let mut max_pos = 0;\n        let mut max_val = i32::MIN;\n        let mut i = 0;\n        while i < b.len() {\n            if b[i] >= low && b[i] <= high {\n                if !found || b[i] > max_val {\n                    max_val = b[i];\n                    max_pos = i;\n                    found = true;\n                }\n            }\n            i += 1;\n        }\n        \n        // Rotate b to bring max to top\n        if max_pos <= b.len() / 2 {\n            for _ in 0..max_pos { rb(b); }\n        } else {\n            for _ in 0..(b.len() - max_pos) { rrb(b); }\n        }\n        \n        // pa\n        pa(b, a);\n        remaining -= 1;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, but this finds the largest element in the chunk and pa's it. Then it finds the second largest, etc. This is correct.\n\nBut the issue is that after pa-ing an element, the remaining elements shift. So the positions change. But my code scans the entire b each time, so it finds the correct position.\n\nGood.\n\nBut wait, there's an issue. After pa-ing an element, the chunk size decreases. So the max_pos might be different. But my code scans the entire b each time, so it finds the correct max_pos.\n\nGood.\n\nLet me also think about the efficiency. For each element, I scan the entire b (O(b.len())). Then I rotate b (O(b.len())). Total: O(b.len()) per element. For n=100: 100 * 100 = 10000. That's fine for the internal computation.\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about the `count_in_range` function. It counts the number of elements in the range [low, high].\n\n```rust\nfn count_in_range(a: &[i32], low: i32, high: i32) -> usize {\n    let mut count = 0;\n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i < a.len() {\n        if a[i] >= low && a[i] <= high {\n            count += 1;\n        }\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    count\n}\n```\n\nThis is correct.\n\nNow let me write the complete code. I'll structure it as a single file with all functions.\n\nOne more thing: the spec says \"no format!\". I need to make sure I only use println! with literal strings. Let me check my code...\n\nAll my println! calls use literal strings: println!(\"ra\"), println!(\"rb\"), etc. Good.\n\nAll my eprintln! calls use literal strings: eprintln!(\"Error\"). Good.\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nActually, I realize I should also handle the case where n=1. The spec says:\n\"If the input is empty, print nothing and exit 0.\"\n\"If the input is already sorted ascending, print nothing and exit 0.\"\n\nFor n=1, the input is already sorted. So I should return immediately.\n\nMy `is_sorted` function returns true for n=1 (the loop doesn't execute). So the code returns immediately. Good.\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nLet me also think about the edge case where all elements are the same. The spec says:\n\"Duplicates are not allowed.\"\n\nSo this case should result in an error. My code checks for duplicates during parsing. Good.\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nOK, I've spent way too much time analyzing. Let me just write the code and test it.\n\nHere's my plan:\n1. Write push_swap.rs with the chunk-based algorithm.\n2. Write Makefile.\n3. Build and test.\n\nLet me write the code now.\n\nFor the chunk-based algorithm, I'll use:\n- 5 chunks for n <= 100\n- 9 chunks for n > 100\n\nThe algorithm:\n1. Push elements to b chunk by chunk (smallest chunk first).\n2. For each element, rotate a to bring it to the top (choosing the direction that minimizes rotations).\n3. After all elements are in b, push them back.\n4. For the push-back phase, process chunks in reverse order (largest first).\n5. Within each chunk, find the largest element, rotate b to bring it to top, pa it. Repeat.\n6. Final rotation to put min on top.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nActually wait, I realize there might be an issue with duplicate values in the chunk boundaries. If sorted[start] == sorted[end-1], the chunk has only one unique value. But since duplicates are not allowed, this shouldn't happen.\n\nBut wait, if n is not divisible by n_chunks, some chunks might have different sizes. For example, n=100, n_chunks=5: each chunk has 20 elements. n=101, n_chunks=5: chunks have 20, 20, 20, 20, 21 elements.\n\nMy code handles this correctly: `start = chunk * n / n_chunks`, `end = (chunk + 1) * n / n_chunks`.\n\nFor n=101, n_chunks=5:\n- Chunk 0: start=0, end=20. 20 elements.\n- Chunk 1: start=20, end=40. 20 elements.\n- Chunk 2: start=40, end=60. 20 elements.\n- Chunk 3: start=60, end=80. 20 elements.\n- Chunk 4: start=80, end=101. 21 elements.\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about the efficiency of the `find_in_range` function. It scans the entire a to find the first element in the range. This is O(a.len()) per call. For n=100, this is 100 * 100 = 10000. That's fine.\n\nBut I can optimize by finding the closest element in the range (minimizing rotation cost). Let me add this optimization.\n\n```rust\nfn find_best_in_range(a: &[i32], low: i32, high: i32) -> (usize, bool) {\n    // Returns (position, is_ra) where is_ra indicates whether to use ra or rra.\n    let mut best_pos = a.len();\n    let mut best_cost = a.len();\n    let mut best_is_ra = true;\n    \n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i < a.len() {\n        if a[i] >= low && a[i] <= high {\n            let cost_ra = i;\n            let cost_rra = a.len() - i;\n            if cost_ra < cost_rra {\n                if cost_ra <