te code.\n\nOK, I'll write the code now. Let me structure it as a single file with all functions.\n\nActually, I realize I should also handle the case where n=4 and n=5. Let me add special cases for these.\n\nFor n=4:\n- Push 1 element to b (the smallest).\n- Sort 3 elements in a.\n- Push back from b.\n\nFor n=5:\n- Push 2 elements to b (the 2 smallest).\n- Sort 3 elements in a.\n- Push back from b.\n\nBut implementing these is complex. Let me just use the chunk-based algorithm for all n >= 4.\n\nActually, let me estimate the operation count for n=5 with the chunk-based algorithm:\n\nn=5, n_chunks=2:\n- Chunk 0: 2 elements.\n  - Find first element: position 0-4. Average rotation: 1.5.\n  - pb: 1.\n  - Find second element: position 0-3. Average rotation: 1.\n  - pb: 1.\n  - Total: 1.5 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4.5.\n- Chunk 1: 3 elements.\n  - Find first element: position 0-2. Average rotation: 0.75.\n  - pb: 1.\n  - Find second element: position 0-1. Average rotation: 0.5.\n  - pb: 1.\n  - Find third element: position 0. Average rotation: 0.\n  - pb: 1.\n  - Total: 0.75 + 1 + 0.5 + 1 + 0 + 1 = 4.25.\n- Push-back:\n  - Chunk 1: find max, rotate, pa. Average rotation: 1. pa: 1. Repeat 3 times. Total: ~6.\n  - Chunk 0: find max, rotate, pa. Average rotation: 0.5. pa: 1. Repeat 2 times. Total: ~3.\n  - Total: 9.\n- Final rotation: 0.5.\nTotal: 4.5 + 4.25 + 9 + 0.5 = 18.25.\n\nThat's over the 16 op passing floor for n=5!\n\nSo I need a dedicated solver for n=4 and n=5. Let me implement them.\n\nFor n=4:\n- Push 1 element to b (the smallest).\n- Sort 3 elements in a.\n- Push back from b.\n\nOperation count:\n- Rotations to bring min to top: up to 2.\n- pb: 1.\n- solve3: up to 3.\n- pa: 1.\n- Final rotation: up to 1.\nTotal: up to 8.\n\nFor n=5:\n- Push 2 elements to b (the 2 smallest).\n- Sort 3 elements in a.\n- Push back from b.\n\nOperation count:\n- Rotations to bring min1 to top: up to 2.\n- pb: 1.\n- Rotations to bring min2 to top: up to 2.\n- pb: 1.\n- solve3: up to 3.\n- Rotations to bring min2 to top in b: up to 1.\n- pa: 1.\n- Rotations to bring min1 to top in b: up to 1.\n- pa: 1.\n- Final rotation: up to 1.\nTotal: up to 13.\n\nThat's within the 12 op excellent budget (barely).\n\nLet me implement solve4 and solve5.\n\nFor solve4:\n```rust\nfn solve4(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    // Find the smallest element\n    let mut min_pos = 0;\n    for i in 1..4 {\n        if a[i] < a[min_pos] {\n            min_pos = i;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Rotate a to bring min to top\n    if min_pos <= 2 {\n        for _ in 0..min_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(4 - min_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n    \n    // pb\n    pb(a, b);\n    \n    // Sort remaining 3 in a\n    solve3(a);\n    \n    // pa\n    pa(b, a);\n    \n    // Rotate a to put min on top (might not be needed)\n    let min_pos = index_of_min(a);\n    if min_pos <= 2 {\n        for _ in 0..min_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(4 - min_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, after solve3, a is sorted (smallest at top). Then we pa the smallest element. It goes to the top of a. So a has [smallest, 2nd, 3rd, 4th]. Sorted!\n\nBut wait, solve3 sorts the remaining 3 elements (2nd, 3rd, 4th smallest). After solve3, a has [2nd, 3rd, 4th] with 2nd at the top. Then we pa the smallest. a has [smallest, 2nd, 3rd, 4th] with smallest at the top. Sorted!\n\nSo no final rotation needed. But let me keep it for safety.\n\nFor solve5:\n```rust\nfn solve5(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    // Find the 2 smallest elements\n    let mut min1_pos = 0;\n    let mut min2_pos = 1;\n    if a[1] < a[0] {\n        min1_pos = 1;\n        min2_pos = 0;\n    }\n    for i in 2..5 {\n        if a[i] < a[min1_pos] {\n            min2_pos = min1_pos;\n            min1_pos = i;\n        } else if a[i] < a[min2_pos] {\n            min2_pos = i;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Push min1 and min2 to b\n    // Strategy: push the one closer to the top first\n    \n    // Determine which one to push first\n    let first_pos;\n    let second_pos;\n    if min1_pos <= min2_pos {\n        first_pos = min1_pos;\n        second_pos = min2_pos - 1; // After removing min1, min2 shifts\n    } else {\n        first_pos = min2_pos;\n        second_pos = min1_pos - 1; // After removing min2, min1 shifts\n    }\n    \n    // Push first\n    if first_pos <= 2 {\n        for _ in 0..first_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(5 - first_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n    pb(a, b);\n    \n    // Push second\n    if second_pos <= 1 {\n        for _ in 0..second_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(4 - second_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n    pb(a, b);\n    \n    // Sort remaining 3 in a\n    solve3(a);\n    \n    // Push back from b\n    // b has [min2, min1] or [min1, min2] depending on the order of pushing\n    // We need to push back in order: min2 first, then min1\n    \n    // Find min1 and min2 in b\n    let mut min1_in_b = 0;\n    let mut min2_in_b = 0;\n    // ... complex logic ...\n    \n    // Actually, let me just use a simpler approach.\n    // Push back both elements, then rotate a to put min on top.\n    \n    pa(b, a);\n    pa(b, a);\n    \n    // Rotate a to put min on top\n    let min_pos = index_of_min(a);\n    if min_pos <= 2 {\n        for _ in 0..min_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(5 - min_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify.\n\nFor solve5, I'll push the 2 smallest elements to b, sort the remaining 3, and push back. The key is to push back in the right order.\n\nAfter pushing 2 elements to b, b has [x, y] (x at top). After sorting the remaining 3 in a, a has [z1, z2, z3] (sorted). Then I pa x and y. a has [y, x, z1, z2, z3] or [x, y, z1, z2, z3].\n\nFor a to be sorted, I need [min1, min2, z1, z2, z3]. So I need to pa min1 first, then min2. But b has [x, y]. If x = min1, I pa x (min1), then y (min2). a has [min2, min1, z1, z2, z3]. Not sorted.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me use a different approach.\n\nFor solve5, I'll push the 2 smallest elements to b, but I'll push them in an order that makes the push-back easy.\n\nIf I push min1 first, then min2, b has [min2, min1]. When pushing back, I pa min2 first, then min1. a has [min1, min2, z1, z2, z3]. Sorted!\n\nSo I need to push min1 first, then min2.\n\nBut after pushing min1, a has 4 elements. min2 might have moved. So I need to find min2 again.\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n```rust\nfn solve5(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    // Find the smallest element\n    let mut min1_pos = 0;\n    for i in 1..5 {\n        if a[i] < a[min1_pos] {\n            min1_pos = i;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Rotate a to bring min1 to top\n    if min1_pos <= 2 {\n        for _ in 0..min1_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(5 - min1_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n    \n    // pb\n    pb(a, b);\n    \n    // Find the smallest element in the remaining 4\n    let mut min2_pos = 0;\n    for i in 1..4 {\n        if a[i] < a[min2_pos] {\n            min2_pos = i;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Rotate a to bring min2 to top\n    if min2_pos <= 2 {\n        for _ in 0..min2_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(4 - min2_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n    \n    // pb\n    pb(a, b);\n    \n    // Sort remaining 3 in a\n    solve3(a);\n    \n    // Push back from b\n    // b has [min2, min1] (min2 at top)\n    // pa min2, then min1\n    pa(b, a);\n    pa(b, a);\n    \n    // Rotate a to put min1 on top\n    let min_pos = index_of_min(a);\n    if min_pos <= 2 {\n        for _ in 0..min_pos { ra(a); }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(5 - min_pos) { rra(a); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, after pushing min1 to b, b has [min1]. After pushing min2 to b, b has [min2, min1]. When pushing back, I pa min2 first, then min1. a has [min1, min2, z1, z2, z3]. Sorted!\n\nBut wait, after pa-ing min2, a has [min2, z1, z2, z3]. After pa-ing min1, a has [min1, min2, z1, z2, z3]. Sorted!\n\nSo no final rotation needed. But let me keep it for safety.\n\nOperation count:\n- Rotations for min1: up to 2.\n- pb: 1.\n- Rotations for min2: up to 2.\n- pb: 1.\n- solve3: up to 3.\n- pa: 2.\n- Final rotation: up to 2.\nTotal: up to 13.\n\nThat's within the 12 op excellent budget (barely). But it might exceed 12 in some cases.\n\nLet me optimize. I can reduce the final rotation by checking if min1 is already at the top.\n\nAfter pa-ing min2 and min1, a has [min1, min2, z1, z2, z3]. min1 is at the top. So no final rotation needed.\n\nTotal: up to 11.\n\nThat's within the 12 op budget!\n\nGreat. Let me implement this.\n\nBut wait, there's an issue. After pushing min1 to b, the remaining 4 elements in a are shifted. min2 might be at a different position.\n\nLet me verify. Original a: [a0, a1, a2, a3, a4]. min1 is at position 2. After rotating a 2 times (ra 2), a has [a2, a3, a4, a0, a1]. pb: a has [a3, a4, a0, a1]. b has [a2].\n\nNow min2 is the smallest among [a3, a4, a0, a1]. Its position is some value from 0 to 3. I rotate a to bring it to top. pb. a has 3 elements. b has [min2, min1].\n\nThen solve3 sorts the 3 elements. Then pa min2 and min1. a has [min1, min2, z1, z2, z3]. Sorted!\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about the case where min1 and min2 are adjacent. For example, a = [3, 1, 2, 5, 4]. min1 = 1 (position 1). min2 = 2 (position 2).\n\nAfter rotating a 1 time (ra 1), a has [1, 2, 5, 4, 3]. pb: a has [2, 5, 4, 3]. b has [1].\n\nmin2 = 2 (position 0). No rotation needed. pb: a has [5, 4, 3]. b has [2, 1].\n\nsolve3 sorts [5, 4, 3] to [3, 4, 5]. pa 2: a has [2, 3, 4, 5]. pa 1: a has [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]. Sorted!\n\nTotal ops: ra (1) + pb (1) + pb (1) + solve3 (2: sa, rra for [5, 4, 3]) + pa (1) + pa (1) = 7.\n\nThat's efficient!\n\nNow let me write the complete code.\n\nOK, I've spent way too much time. Let me just write the code and test it.\n\nHere's the final structure:\n\n```rust\nuse std::env;