`\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me look at a simpler but effective approach.\n\nActually, let me use a well-tested approach. Here's a clean implementation:\n\n```rust\nfn solve(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let n = a.len();\n    \n    if n <= 1 {\n        return;\n    }\n    if n == 2 {\n        if a[0] > a[1] {\n            println!(\"sa\");\n        }\n        return;\n    }\n    if n == 3 {\n        solve3(a);\n        return;\n    }\n    if n == 5 {\n        solve5(a, b);\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    // For larger inputs, use chunk-based sorting\n    let n_chunks = if n <= 100 { 5 } else { 9 };\n    \n    // Find sorted order to determine chunk boundaries\n    let mut sorted = vec_clone(a);\n    insertion_sort(&mut sorted);\n    \n    // Push to b using chunks\n    let mut pushed = 0;\n    while pushed < n {\n        let mut found = false;\n        for chunk in 0..n_chunks {\n            let start = chunk * n / n_chunks;\n            let end = (chunk + 1) * n / n_chunks;\n            let low = sorted[start];\n            let high = sorted[end - 1];\n            \n            // Find element in this chunk range\n            let (pos, dir) = find_best_rotate(a, |x| x >= low && x <= high);\n            \n            if pos < a.len() {\n                // Rotate to position\n                apply_rotate(a, pos, dir);\n                println!(\"pb\");\n                let val = a.remove(0);\n                b.insert(0, val);\n                pushed += 1;\n                found = true;\n            }\n        }\n        if !found {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Push back from b to a\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nWait, I'm overcomplicating this. Let me think about what operations are most efficient.\n\nThe key insight for push_swap is:\n- `ra`/`rra` cost 1 each\n- `pb` costs 1\n- So to push an element at position k from the top:\n  - If k < n/2: use k `ra` operations\n  - If k >= n/2: use (n-k) `rra` operations\n\nFor the chunk