now. I'll use a chunk-based algorithm with the following steps:\n\n1. Parse and validate input\n2. If empty or sorted, return\n3. For n=2: swap if needed\n4. For n=3: use lookup table\n5. For n>=4: chunk-based algorithm\n   a. Push elements to b chunk by chunk\n   b. Push back from b to a chunk by chunk\n   c. Rotate a to put min on top\n\nFor the chunk-based algorithm, I'll use a simple approach without positioning in b. I'll test it and see how it performs.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\n```rust\nuse std::env;\nuse std::process;\n\nfn main() {\n    let args: Vec<String> = env::args().skip(1).collect();\n    \n    if args.is_empty() {\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    let mut numbers: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    \n    for arg in &args {\n        if arg.is_empty() {\n            eprintln!(\"Error\");\n            process::exit(1);\n        }\n        for token in arg.split_whitespace() {\n            let val = match parse_i32(token) {\n                Some(v) => v,\n                None => {\n                    eprintln!(\"Error\");\n                    process::exit(1);\n                }\n            };\n            // Check duplicates\n            let mut is_dup = false;\n            for existing in &numbers {\n                if *existing == val {\n                    is_dup = true;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n            if is_dup {\n                eprintln!(\"Error\");\n                process::exit(1);\n            }\n            numbers.push(val);\n        }\n    }\n    \n    if numbers.is_empty() {\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    if is_sorted(&numbers) {\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    let mut stack_a = numbers;\n    let mut stack_b: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();\n    \n    let len = stack_a.len();\n    \n    if len == 2 {\n        // swap if needed\n        // Actually, we need to check\n        if stack_a[0] > stack_a[1] {\n            println!(\"sa\");\n        }\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    if len == 3 {\n        solve3(&mut stack_a);\n        return;\n    }\n    \n    solve_large(&mut stack_a, &mut stack_b, len);\n}\n\nfn parse_i32(s: &str) -> Option<i32> {\n    let bytes = s.as_bytes();\n    if bytes.is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    \n    let mut i = 0;\n    let mut negative = false;\n    \n    if bytes[0] == b'+' {\n        i = 1;\n        if i >= bytes.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n    } else if bytes[0] == b'-' {\n        negative = true;\n        i = 1;\n        if i >= bytes.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    let mut result: i32 = 0;\n    \n    while i < bytes.len() {\n        let digit = bytes[i];\n        if digit < b'0' || digit > b'9' {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let d = (digit - b'0') as i32;\n        \n        if negative {\n            if result < i32::MIN / 10 || (result == i32::MIN / 10 && d > 8) {\n                return None;\n            }\n        } else {\n            if result > i32::MAX / 10 || (result == i32::MAX / 10 && d > 7) {\n                return None;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        result = result * 10 + d;\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    \n    if negative {\n        Some(-result)\n    } else {\n        Some(result)\n    }\n}\n\nfn is_sorted(a: &[i32]) -> bool {\n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i + 1 < a.len() {\n        if a[i] > a[i + 1] {\n            return false;\n        }\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    true\n}\n\nfn solve3(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let x = a[0];\n    let y = a[1];\n    let z = a[2];\n    \n    if x < y && y < z {\n        // Already sorted\n    } else if x > y && y < z && x > z {\n        // [3, 1, 2] -> ra\n        println!(\"ra\");\n    } else if x < y && y > z && x < z {\n        // [1, 3, 2] -> sa, ra\n        println!(\"sa\");\n        println!(\"ra\");\n    } else if x > y && y < z && x < z {\n        // [2, 1, 3] -> sa\n        println!(\"sa\");\n    } else if x > y && y > z {\n        // [3, 2, 1] -> sa, rra\n        println!(\"sa\");\n        println!(\"rra\");\n    } else if x < y && y > z && x > z {\n        // [2, 3, 1] -> rra\n        println!(\"rra\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn solve_large(a: &mut Vec<i32>, b: &mut Vec<i32>, n: usize) {\n    let n_chunks = if n <= 100 { 5 } else { 9 };\n    \n    // Get sorted copy\n    let mut sorted: Vec<i32> = Vec::with_capacity(n);\n    for x in a.iter() {\n        sorted.push(*x);\n    }\n    insertion_sort(&mut sorted);\n    \n    // Push to b, chunk by chunk\n    for chunk in 0..n_chunks {\n        let start = chunk * n / n_chunks;\n        let end = (chunk + 1) * n / n_chunks;\n        let low = sorted[start];\n        let high = sorted[end - 1];\n        \n        loop {\n            let pos = find_in_range(a, low, high);\n            if pos >= a.len() {\n                break;\n            }\n            \n            if pos <= a.len() / 2 {\n                for _ in 0..pos {\n                    ra(a);\n                }\n            } else {\n                for _ in 0..(a.len() - pos) {\n                    rra(a);\n                }\n            }\n            \n            let val = a.remove(0);\n            b.insert(0, val);\n            println!(\"pb\");\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Push back from b to a, chunk by chunk (largest first)\n    for chunk in (0..n_chunks).rev() {\n        let start = chunk * n / n_chunks;\n        let end = (chunk + 1) * n / n_chunks;\n        let low = sorted[start];\n        let high = sorted[end - 1];\n        \n        loop {\n            let pos = find_in_range(b, low, high);\n            if pos >= b.len() {\n                break;\n            }\n            \n            if pos <= b.len() / 2 {\n                for _ in 0..pos {\n                    rb(b);\n                }\n            } else {\n                for _ in 0..(b.len() - pos) {\n                    rrb(b);\n                }\n            }\n            \n            let val = b.remove(0);\n            a.insert(0, val);\n            println!(\"pa\");\n        }\n    }\n    \n    // Rotate a to put min on top\n    let min_pos = index_of_min(a);\n    if min_pos <= a.len() / 2 {\n        for _ in 0..min_pos {\n            ra(a);\n        }\n    } else {\n        for _ in 0..(a.len() - min_pos) {\n            rra(a);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn find_in_range(a: &[i32], low: i32, high: i32) -> usize {\n    let mut i = 0;\n    while i < a.len() {\n        if a[i] >= low && a[i] <= high {\n            return i;\n        }\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    a.len()\n}\n\nfn index_of_min(a: &[i32]) -> usize {\n    let mut min_pos = 0;\n    let mut i = 1;\n    while i < a.len() {\n        if a[i] < a[min_pos] {\n            min_pos = i;\n        }\n        i += 1;\n    }\n    min_pos\n}\n\nfn insertion_sort(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    let mut i = 1;\n    while i < a.len() {\n        let key = a[i];\n        let mut j = i;\n        while j > 0 && a[j - 1] > key {\n            a[j] = a[j - 1];\n            j -= 1;\n        }\n        a[j] = key;\n        i += 1;\n    }\n}\n\nfn ra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let first = a[0];\n        let mut i = 0;\n        while i + 1 < a.len() {\n            a[i] = a[i + 1];\n            i += 1;\n        }\n        a[a.len() - 1] = first;\n        println!(\"ra\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn rra(a: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if a.len() >= 2 {\n        let last = a[a.len() - 1];\n        let mut i = a.len() - 1;\n        while i > 0 {\n            a[i] = a[i - 1];\n            i -= 1;\n        }\n        a[0] = last;\n        println!(\"rra\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn rb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        let first = b[0];\n        let mut i = 0;\n        while i + 1 < b.len() {\n            b[i] = b[i + 1];\n            i += 1;\n        }\n        b[b.len() - 1] = first;\n        println!(\"rb\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn rrb(b: &mut Vec<i32>) {\n    if b.len() >= 2 {\n        let last = b[b.len() - 1];\n        let mut i = b.len() - 1;\n        while i > 0 {\n            b[i] = b[i - 1];\n            i -= 1;\n        }\n        b[0] = last;\n        println!(\"rrb\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWait, I'm using `a.remove(0)` and `a.insert(0, val)` which are O(n) operations. But that's fine for the algorithm's correctness. The operation count is about the printed operations, not the internal computation.\n\nHowever, I realize that using `remove(0)` and `insert(0, ...)` is inefficient for the internal computation, but it doesn't affect the output. The output is correct as long as the stack operations are correct.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the stack representation. The spec says \"top of the stack = first argument\". So if the input is `3 1 4`, stack a starts as [3, 1, 4] with 3 at the top (index 0).\n\nWhen we `pb`, we take the top element of a (index 0) and push it onto b (at index 0). So b becomes [3] with 3 at the top.\n\nWhen we `pa`, we take the top element of b (index 0) and push it onto a (at index 0).\n\nWhen we `ra`, we rotate a up: the first element becomes the last. So [3, 1, 4] becomes [1, 4, 3].\n\nWhen we `rra`, we reverse rotate: the last element becomes the first. So [3, 1, 4] becomes [4, 3, 1].\n\nThis matches my implementation. Good.\n\nNow, let me think about the efficiency of the algorithm. For n=100 with 5 chunks:\n\nPush phase:\n- For each element, we rotate a to find it and then pb.\n- The rotation cost depends on the position of the element in a.\n- As we push elements, a gets smaller, so the rotation cost decreases.\n\nLet me estimate more carefully. For the first chunk (smallest 20 elements):\n- These elements are scattered throughout a.\n- On average, each element is at position 50 (half of 100).\n- But we choose the direction (ra or rra) to minimize rotations, so average cost is 25.\n- Total for first chunk: 20 * 25 = 500 rotations + 20 pb = 520 ops.\n\nFor the second chunk:\n- a now has 80 elements.\n- Average rotation cost: 20.\n- Total: 20 * 20 = 400 rotations + 20 pb = 420 ops.\n\nFor the third chunk:\n- a has 60 elements.\n- Average rotation cost: 15.\n- Total: 20 * 15 = 300 rotations + 20 pb = 320 ops.\n\nFor the fourth chunk:\n- a has 40 elements.\n- Average rotation cost: 10.\n- Total: 20 * 10 = 200 rotations + 20 pb = 220 ops.\n\nFor the fifth chunk:\n- a has 20 elements.\n- Average rotation cost: 5.\n- Total: 20 * 5 = 100 rotations + 20 pb = 120 ops.\n\nTotal push phase: 520 + 420 + 320 + 220 + 120 = 1600 ops.\n\nThat's already over the 1500 budget for n=100! And I haven't even counted the push-back phase.\n\nSo I need a better algorithm. Let me think about this.\n\nThe key issue is that elements in a chunk are scattered throughout a, so we need to rotate a a lot to find them.\n\nA better approach: instead of processing chunks in order, process elements in a way that minimizes rotations. For example:\n- Find the element closest to the top of a\n- If it's in the current chunk, pb it\n- Otherwise, rotate a to bring the next element in the current chunk to the top\n\nBut this is what I'm already doing. The issue is that elements in a chunk are scattered.\n\nA different approach: use a \"turbo\" algorithm that pushes elements to b based on their position relative to the median.\n\nActually, let me try a different chunk-based approach. Instead of pushing all elements of a chunk before moving to the next, interleave the chunks:\n\n1. For each pass through a:\n   a. For each chunk (in order):\n      - If the top of a is in this chunk, pb it\n      - Otherwise, ra\n   b. Repeat until all elements are in b\n\nThis way, we rotate a once per element, and pb when the element is in the current chunk. Total rotations: n (one ra per element), plus n pb. Total: 2n = 200 for n=100.\n\nBut wait, this doesn't minimize rotations. We might ra past elements that are in the current chunk.\n\nLet me think differently. The key insight for an efficient push_swap is to minimize the total rotation cost. One way to do this is to process elements in the order they appear in a, rather than by chunk.\n\nHere's the approach:\n1. For each element in a (in order):\n   a. Determine its chunk\n   b. pb it\n   c. Rotate b to position it correctly\n2. After all elements are in b, push them back\n\nThe rotation cost for step 1 is 0 (we just pb the top element). The rotation cost for step 1c is the cost of positioning in b.\n\nFor step 1c, we rotate b to put the element in the right position. The cost depends on the size of b and the position of the element.\n\nFor n=100:\n- Step 1: 100 pb + positioning rotations\n- Positioning rotations: for each element, rotate b to find the right position. Average cost: b.len() / 2.\n- Total positioning: 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + 99 = 4950. Way over budget.\n\nSo this approach is also not efficient.\n\nLet me think about the standard push_swap algorithm more carefully. The key insight is:\n\n1. Push elements to b in chunk order, but also track their position within b.\n2. When pushing back, elements within a chunk are already in order, so minimal rotations.\n\nThe positioning in b is done by rotating b after each pb. The cost of positioning is O(1) per element if we're smart about it.\n\nHere's the idea: when pushing an element to b, rotate b to put the element at a specific position (e.g., the position corresponding to its rank within the chunk). This way, when pushing back, elements within a chunk are already in order.\n\nBut implementing this requires tracking the rank of each element within its chunk, which is complex.\n\nLet me try a simpler approach: use the \"index-based\" algorithm.\n\n1. Assign each element an index (0 = smallest, 1 = second smallest, etc.).\n2. Push elements to b based on their index ranges.\n3. When pushing to b, also rotate b to position elements.\n4. Push back from b to a.\n\nActually, this is similar to the chunk-based approach. Let me just implement it and see how it performs.\n\nOK, I think I've been overthinking this. Let me just implement a working solution with the chunk-based algorithm and test it. If it do